[Music]  April is a special month for this  channel it's the one-year anniversary of  the first video it's the month we first  surpassed 1 million views and it's also  the month that I got to meet a lot of  people in person that I know from this  project at ng-conf in Salt Lake City  this video marks episode 104 we're going  to do something a little bit different  than normal I'm going to spend some time  answering questions that you've asked on  slack and YouTube that wouldn't normally  fit into this channels content but  before we get into that I just wanted to  say thank you to everybody who supports  this channel either with a pro  membership or just by simply liking and  watching the videos  it's your feedback that really drives  the direction of this content and I'm  really grateful for that to show my  appreciation I'm going to provide 10  free t-shirts or gift cards to anybody  that comments on this video with a  suggestion about what they want to see  next just leave a comment and I will  randomly select the winners by the end  of this week okay so now let's jump into  question number one  how was ng calm I have been to quite a  few tech conferences in the past and  most of them are pretty boring but I'd  have to say ng comm stands out as being  extremely well organized and engaging if  you ever get the chance to go it's well  worth the investment  but what I really want to share is my  top five most exciting things happening  in the angular world number one by far  for me is angular elements this allows  us to build angular components and use  them as custom elements in HTML without  bootstrapping an entire angular app this  opens up an entirely new paradigm for  angular development where we can focus  on building components and then drop  them into existing applications for  example if you have a Ruby on Rails  application you can build an angular  component provide a script and then use  that component in your rails app it's a  total game changer for angular  developers number two is the new IV  render engine the first thing it's going  to do is dramatically reduce bundle  sizes with advanced tree shaking  techniques it can Preston minified hello  world app is only 2.7 kilobytes which is  extremely small but if that's not enough  it's also going to dramatically improve  debugging capabilities if you've ever  seen the expression has changed after  it's been checked error then you  probably know that it's very frustrating  to debug but IB includes a much improved  stacktrace that will take you exactly to  the point in the  template where that error occurred so  very much looking forward to IV and  version six I'm also looking forward to  rxjs version six if you're like me your  components probably have twenty  different lines dedicated to rxjs and  ports and that's because there's  hundreds of different import points in  the library but in version six there  will only be two main import locations  which will dramatically reduce the  number of lines of code it takes to use  rx in your angular project now moving on  to number four is the new vs code live  share mode which was announced at  ng-conf this feature allows multiple  people to collaborate on a document in  real time similar to how Google Docs  works this means that you can pair  program remotely on a shared document  which opens up the door for all kinds of  cool stuff with education and just  teamwork in general the last thing I  want to show you is a new feature from  stack blitz called teleport it will show  you real-time changes happening to your  app across all devices so if you're  building a PWA on your desktop and you  want to see how it works on a mobile  device you can do that using teleport  stack blitz will give you a URL that you  can load up on your iPhone and then you  can see the changes happening in real  time similar to how real-time features  work in firebase that wraps up the top  five for me but make sure to watch all  the talks on YouTube because there is so  much great stuff that I just wasn't able  to cover here I want to switch gears to  a question I get asked frequently which  is how do you make your videos the first  thing I do is I write a bunch of code on  my Ubuntu workspace and make sure that I  have a solid working demo before doing  anything else from there I have a  special patented technique that I use  for recording code snippets what I do is  I delete things in reverse order then I  click record on my camera and then hit  control Z or undo to rebuild the  function step-by-step once I have those  recorded I use Adobe Creative Cloud to  produce the video mostly Adobe Premiere  but also Adobe After Effects for certain  animations I create the videos on  Windows but I also have a Mac which is  equally effective at producing videos  the next question I want to address is  what is a Cordova plug-in which was  asked by Hayden on our slack channel  this is a great question because Cordova  plugins basically feel like a black box  and we just kind of trust  that they're doing what they're supposed  to do the underlying problem that  Cordova plugins solve is that we want to  use JavaScript to interact with SDKs  that only understand Java Objective C  Swift and Kotlin  so a Cordova plug-in can be thought of  as a bridge that allows us to use a  JavaScript interface to write code that  normally only runs in these other  languages that native mobile apps  understand if you look at the source  code for a plug-in you'll see that it's  code base is normally split between  JavaScript and then Android and iOS code  you'll generally see a file for iOS  which is going to have some objective-c  files then you'll see an android folder  which generally has some Java files on  my future video list  I have building a capacitor plugin from  scratch which is very similar to Cordova  but that video is probably still a  couple months off the next question  comes from Raul on YouTube I've never  used a state management library should I  learn ng rx or ng XS but I think a much  more important question is do I need a  state management library at all because  both libraries will add additional  overhead and complexity to your  application in an effort to make complex  state more predictable and easy to test  there's a good scene in the react world  that says you don't need redux until you  know you need redux and i think there's  a lot of truth in that I have a  QuickStart lesson for both so test-drive  them and see which one fits your actual  coding style and the Preferences of your  team the final question comes from  Pierre on slack and that's how do I  build an online shop with angular and  firebase the beauty of the angular  firebase stack is that it affords you a  ton of flexibility but at the same time  it helps you build things quickly and  reliably whenever building something  involving payments or e-commerce I  recommend leveraging a third party API  because building this from scratch is  something that's hard to pull off for  most businesses stripe payments for  example gives you a ton of tools to  implement your own e-commerce solution  but there's a bunch of other  alternatives that you should compare and  contrast such as Square Braintree and a  new one called Bolton that I haven't  actually tried but it does look very  interesting what's really interesting is  now that angular elements is on the  horizon it opens up the door to use  angular components and platforms like  Shopify so you could use the underlying  platform for  or e-commerce solution and then add  interactivity on top of it with angular  elements I'm gonna go ahead and wrap  things up here if this video helped you  please like and subscribe and don't  forget to leave a comment for your  chance to win a free t-shirt or gift  card thanks again for your support and I  can't wait to see what the next 100  videos will bring  [Music]

